%I A052157 #35 Nov 26 2026 20:33:10
%S A052157 2,21,1310,10199373,131487690156
%N A052157 Least positive integer r such that there exists an integer s, 0 <= s < r gcd(r-i, s-j) > 1 for all integers i, j with 0 <= i, j < n.
%C A052157 a(n) is the least r of an n X n rhombus on the hexagonal grid of the Kronecker symbol (x, d) with all cells equal to 0, with top corner (x, d) = (r, s), 0 <= s < r. - _Matej Veselovac_, Nov 20 2026
%H A052157 S. Laishram and F. Luca, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL18/Laishram/lai2.html">Rectangles Of Nonvisible Lattice Points</a>, J. Int. Seq. 18 (2015) 15.10.8.
%H A052157 Mathematics Stack Exchange, <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/q/5145414">Visualization of the Kronecker symbol</a>, Jul 29 2026.
%H A052157 Matej Veselovac, <a href="https://vepirson.github.io/kronecker-hex/#/explore?x=131487690156&amp;d=37945747192&amp;z=40&amp;sel=0,0">a(5) on the hexagonal grid of the Kronecker symbol (x, d) = (r, s)</a>, Nov 20 2026.
%F A052157 a_n < e^{(1+o(1)) 2 n^2 log n}.
%e A052157 a(1) = 2 because we can choose r = 2, s = 0; a(2) = 21 because we can choose r = 21, s = 15; a(3) = 1310 because we can choose r = 1310, s = 1276.
%e A052157 a(4) = 10199373: r = 10199373, s = 7247646. - _William Rex Marshall_, Nov 23 2009
%e A052157 a(5) = 131487690156: r = 131487690156, s = 37945747192. - _Matej Veselovac_, Nov 20 2026
%e A052157 a(6) <= 17847964296933353843690 by constructing the pair (r, s) = (17847964296933353843690, 6125780473887169562170). - _Matej Veselovac_, Nov 21 2026
%o A052157 (PARI) is(r,n)=n--;my(s=[Mod(0,1)],f,v,p);for(i=0,n,f=factor(r-i)[,1]; for(j=0,n, v=List(); for(k=1,#f, p=f[k]; for(k=1,#s, if(s[k].mod%p, listput(v,chinese(Mod(j,p),s[k])), if(Mod(s[k],p)==j, listput(v,s[k]))))); s=select(m->lift(m)<r,Set(v)); if(#s==0, return(0)))); s[1]
%o A052157 a(n)=my(r=n); while(!is(r++,n),); r \\ _Charles R Greathouse IV_, Nov 05 2013
%Y A052157 Cf. A325602, A325603 (2 X 2), A325604, A325605 (3 X 3), A325606, A325607 (4 X 4).
%K A052157 nonn,nice,more,changed
%O A052157 1,1
%A A052157 _Jeffrey Shallit_, Jan 25 2000
%E A052157 By brute force search I know that a(4) > 410000. And also I know by constructing the pair (r, s) = (477742707, 172379781) that a(4) <= 477742707.
%E A052157 a(4) > 1475000. - _Jud McCranie_, Jan 26 2000
%E A052157 a(4) from _William Rex Marshall_, Nov 23 2009
%E A052157 a(5) from _Matej Veselovac_, Nov 20 2026
